<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Mount Ebal</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Mount Ebal is a mountain north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>, facing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> across the valley of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrew-bible">Hebrew Bible</a> it is where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> commanded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> to build an altar and pronounce curses, while blessings were spoken from Gerizim. An <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a> stone structure on its slope, excavated in the 1980s, has been debated as a possible early altar.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mount-ebal.jpg" alt="Mount Ebal" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Someone35 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mount_ebal,_near_nablus_4.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Mount Ebal is one of the two mountains near the city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nablus">Nablus</a>, biblical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shechem">Shechem</a>, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/west-bank">West Bank</a>. It forms the northern side of the valley in which Nablus lies, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> forms the southern side. It rises to 935 metres above sea level, some 60 metres higher than Gerizim, is about 17 square kilometres in area and consists mainly of limestone. Its slopes hold several large caverns, probably once quarries, and at the base to the north there are several tombs. On the higher western part are the ruins of massive walls called Al-Kal'ah, and to the east a site called Kunaisah.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In Deuteronomy 11:29 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> directs that, once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/israelites">Israelites</a> enter the land, the blessing is to be put on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-gerizim">Mount Gerizim</a> and the curse on Mount Ebal. In the Masoretic Text and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septuagint">Septuagint</a> version of Deuteronomy 27, there is an instruction to build an altar of uncut stones on Ebal, to whiten stones with lime, to make peace offerings and to write the words of the law on the stones. The Samaritan Pentateuch and a Qumran fragment place this instruction at Mount Gerizim instead, which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samaritans">Samaritans</a> hold holy. Some scholars think the Samaritan reading probably more accurate, and a study of 2018 argued that the Gerizim reading is older and the Ebal reading a later, polemical revision.</p><p>The tribes of Simeon, Levi, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/judah">Judah</a>, Issachar, Joseph and Benjamin were to stand on Gerizim, and Reuben, Gad, Asher, Zebulun, Dan and Naphtali on Ebal, where twelve curses were pronounced by the Levite priesthood and answered by the people with Amen. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-joshua">Book of Joshua</a>, after the Battle of Ai, Joshua builds an altar of unhewn stones, the Law of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moses">Moses</a> is written on stones and the people pronounce blessings and curses as Deuteronomy instructs. Scholars debate whether that account joins two earlier ones, and generally hold that Joshua's sources are older than Deuteronomy.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In 1980 the Israeli archaeologist Adam Zertal discovered a structure on Mount Ebal, at northern el-Burnat, during the Manasseh Hill Country Survey. The University of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haifa">Haifa</a> and the Israel Exploration Society excavated it over eight seasons from 1982 to 1989 and found scarabs, seals and animal bones of the Iron Age I period. Most archaeologists now agree that it was a site of early Israelite cultic activity. Zertal proposed that it was the altar that Joshua built to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yhwh">Yahweh</a>, where the Covenant was renewed, but a number of archaeologists have disputed this identification.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Mount Ebal” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shechem, at Tell Balata near modern Nablus, commanded the pass between Mount Gerizim and <b>Mount Ebal</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/shechem">Shechem</a></li></ul></section>
